  "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title><jats:p>Simulation of a passively aerated biological filter packed with non‐woven polyester fabric (NWPF) was carried out using GPS‐X V.7 software. System was operated at HLR and OLR rates of 11.1 m<jats:sup>3</jats:sup>/m<jats:sup>2</jats:sup>. d and 2.5 kg BOD m<jats:sup>3</jats:sup>. d, respectively. Calibration of experimental results indicated excellent correlation between experimental and modelled effluent. Simulation was done by incremental increase of HLR, OLR until break through point. System was very efficient at OLR up to 4.1 kg BOD/m<jats:sup>3</jats:sup>. d where removal rates of 81–91% COD and 86–97% TSS were achieved. Increasing OLR to 2.25 folds of its original value (5.7 kg BOD/m<jats:sup>3</jats:sup>. d) decreased the efficiency of the system by 55% COD and 37% TSS. Increasing surface area of the packing material increased the overall efficiency of the treatment system. This study proved that simulation of any project prior implementation is a better optimization tool for design and construction as well as reducing costs of laboratory analyses.</jats:p>",
